<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Territory of Western Samoa</h1><p class="st-meta">1914 CE – 1962 CE · capital Apia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">New Zealand's rule over the former German Samoa, marked by a deadly influenza epidemic and the Mau protests, which ended in 1962 as the first independent Pacific island nation.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> troops occupied <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/german-samoa">German Samoa</a> in August 1914, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain's</a> request, and governed it under military rule for the rest of the war. Afterwards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> a mandate over the territory, governed from 1920 by a civil administrator at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apia">Apia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In 1918 the ship Talune brought influenza that killed about a fifth of the population, while <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/american-samoa">American Samoa</a> escaped through quarantine. Resentment fed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mau-movement">Mau movement</a> of non-cooperation, led by the merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-frederick-nelson">Olaf Frederick Nelson</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupua-tamasese-lealofi-iii">Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III</a>. On 28 December 1929, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-saturday-1929">Black Saturday</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> police fired on a Mau procession in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apia">Apia</a>, killing Tamasese and others. The territory became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> trust territory in 1946.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Self-government advanced in the 1950s under Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiame-mataafa-faumuina-mulinuu-ii">Fiamē Mataʻafa</a>. Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samoan-islands">Samoa</a> became independent on 1 January 1962, the first Pacific island state to do so, and was renamed Samoa in 1997. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> formally apologised for its colonial wrongs in 2002.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/territory-of-western-samoa.jpg" alt="Territory of Western Samoa" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Original: New Zealand Government Vector: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Samoa_Trust_Territory.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1914 CE – 1962 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Apia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>George Spafford Richardson, Fiamē Mataʻafa Faumuina Mulinuʻu II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism, Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Samoan, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/samoa">Samoa</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Western Samoa, Western Samoa Trust Territory</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1938&amp;focus=territory-of-western-samoa" aria-label="Territory of Western Samoa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="-25 2693 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Territory of Western Samoa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M295 2949L293 2949M295 2949L9268 3906M295 2949L9268 3906"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M293 2949h0M9268 3906h0M9268 3906h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M295 2949h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M295 2949h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1938&amp;focus=territory-of-western-samoa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
